# What may enter a Docker build context. **An acceptance list, never an exclusion list.**
#
# ⚠️⚠️ THIS IS THE SECOND GUARD ON THE RULE THAT MATTERS MOST HERE. `forge/` must never reach the VPS
# -- it holds the generator and the human-solvability guarantee, which is trade secret (CLAUDE.md
# sections 16 and 33). **A build context is a copy**: `docker build` ships the whole context to the
# daemon, and `deploy/serve.Dockerfile` is built with the repository root as its context. Without this
# file that context is the entire repository, `forge/` and `forge/inventory/` included.
#
# The first guard is that the build happens on the laptop and only the finished image is transferred
# (`tools/create_docker_image.ps1`). This is what holds when somebody builds somewhere else.
#
# ⚠️ **Everything is excluded and only named directories are admitted**, which is the form this
# project requires wherever absence must not read as permission. The consequence is the point: a
# directory added next year is outside the context **by not being named**, rather than inside it by
# not having been forbidden. An exclusion list would have to be updated by whoever adds the directory,
# and they will not know that it needs updating.
#
# Adding a line here means deciding that its contents may travel into an image. There are exactly two.

*

# The client: the pages, the engine, the fonts, and the two public packages. This is the site.
!serve/

# The Dockerfiles and the nginx template. `serve.Dockerfile` copies the template from here, which is
# the only reason the context has to be the root rather than `serve/` itself.
!deploy/
